spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:75:## 6. User journeys
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:89:## 7. Functional requirements
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:96:| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order 
is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the 
conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or 
when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after 
fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt that started before or during the outage is superseded 
immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays 
the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | 
Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:98:| FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the 
item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). 
The server accepts a create only if its generation is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the 
moment of commit, and a fence with generation g atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as 
delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to commit — including requests still in the network that have not 
reached the server; a fence or create with a generation older than the server's current one is rejected. When an 
attempt ends without a usable response (network error, timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the 
item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a 
message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:99:| FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic 
attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the 
same conversation wait until it is resolved; other conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, 
"Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new `clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:103:| FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an 
application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch and fence call the app checks that the authenticated user equals 
the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected sender, and the create and fence routes reject (without storing 
or fencing) a request whose authenticated user differs. When the authenticated user changes or signs out in any tab, 
all drains in all tabs stop; create, fence, and lookup responses that arrive afterwards are discarded: they never add 
content to another user's view, never resurrect deleted items, and never change a newer claim. Under the same 
identity, a response whose attempt generation is older than the item's current generation, or for an item already 
cancelled/deleted, is likewise ignored. | Must | AC-012, AC-044, AC-047 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:116:| FR-024 | On first creation the server stores mentions in the 
same transaction as the message, as application user IDs of conversation members at that time other than the sender; 
other requested IDs create no mention. The original submitted composition (content, reply target, requested mention 
IDs) is what replays are compared against: an unchanged replay returns the stored message without re-validating 
recipients; a different composition under the same key returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`. Clients cannot write 
mention records directly; only conversation members can read them. | Must | AC-024, AC-046 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:126:| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations 
of a message (outbox item, create and fence responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and 
the feed matches pending items to stored messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in 
every sender tab, including identical texts and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:128:## 8. Business rules
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:132:| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user 
(`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, 
and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; 
reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:135:| BR-004 | Each item gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the 
user presses send; every attempt reuses it (except the `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` retry, FR-007); the server stores at 
most one message per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`), and a fenced attempt can never commit. | R1-q2 B, 
R1-q16 B; review r3 #1 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:136:| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before 
dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network 
error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports 
signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 
(e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 
4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is 
ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #2, #11 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:141:| BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation 
at first creation, other than the sender, can be mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no 
`@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:145:| BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the 
phase (written + applied locally with recorded rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` 
only with explicit owner authorization, followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is 
deployed by the owner after the migrations; rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
